# Design Sync Workflow Rules

## Intent

Define exactly how design flows from the Replit design repo into this app repo without
either agent overstepping — i.e. without a sync ever deleting a design the owner wants to keep, and
without the app agent ever editing or pushing to the design repo. Companion rules: `126-` (what to
do once a design exists) and `127-` (when to stop and wait for a design). This rule (`128-`) covers
the mechanics of the sync itself.

## Two Repos, One Direction

- Design repo (`https://github.com/chargingthefuture/design`, vendored here as the `design/`
  submodule, authored by the Replit design agent) — owns mockup components, the landing page, the
  slideshow deck, and design tokens.
- App repo (this repo) — owns production application code, backend, schema, infra.

```
design-repo  ──(reference only, one way)──▶  app-repo
```

The app repo never writes back to the design repo. The design agent never reaches into app
business logic. This one-directional rule is what makes a sync safe: a sync is a *pull of a known
pointer*, never a "make both sides match" reconciliation that could delete wanted work.

## A mockup is a pre-build artifact, not a living mirror (production-era, 2026-06-05)

A design sync feeds the **first build** of a surface. It is **not** a mandate to keep a shipped
screen byte-identical to its mockup forever. Once a screen is in production, the **running screen is
the source of truth** for that screen (see rule `127-` "Production-era policy"), and the owner / app
agent iterates on it directly in code. The matching mockup is then effectively frozen — it will drift
from the live screen, and that drift is **expected and fine**; do not "re-sync" a live screen back to
its old mockup, and do not file design-queue prompts to make a mockup match a screen that already
shipped. The sync mechanics below apply when adopting a **new** mockup (a net-new surface, or a
deliberate from-scratch redesign), not for iterating on what already ships.

## How The App Consumes Designs: Submodule-Pin (NOT file-copy)

This app does not copy mockup `.tsx` files into its own tree. There is no
`ctf/.../components/mockups/` directory and there must not be one. The app vendors the entire design
repo as the `design/` git submodule and:

1. Pins a specific design-repo commit via the submodule pointer.
2. Implements separate production shells (e.g. `ctf/packages/web/components/clicklog/clicklog-shell.tsx`)
   that are *informed by* the mockups in `design/`, per rule `126-`.

> The design repo's `sync-manifest.json` describes a `dest_dir` of
> `<APP_REPO>/src/components/mockups/survivor-hub` and a file-copy + `// design-sync` marker model.
> That file-copy model is for a hypothetical app that vendors mockups directly. This app does
> not. Here the manifest and the `// design-sync` markers serve a different, still-useful purpose:
> they are the design-side deletion guard (see below) and the authoritative *list* of which
> mockup files are live. The app reads mockups in place from the submodule at
> `design/artifacts/mockup-sandbox/src/components/mockups/survivor-hub/`; it never copies them.

## App-Side Sync Procedure (read-only against the design repo)

Run the helper or the equivalent commands. None of these write to the design repo.

```bash
ctf/scripts/sync-design.sh        # fetch + update --remote + show what changed
```

Equivalent manual steps:

1. `git -C design fetch` — see what the Replit agent has pushed (the in-tree pointer often lags).
2. `git -C design log --oneline -10` — review new design commits.
3. `git submodule update --remote design` — advance the local checkout to the design remote HEAD.
4. `git add design && git commit` — record the new pinned design SHA in the app repo. The commit
   message should name the design commits being adopted.
5. Implement / update production shells against the new mockups per rule `126-`. UI gated by `127-`.

A "design sync" in this repo is steps 1–4: bumping the pinned design pointer. It copies no
mockup files, deletes nothing, and touches nothing under `design/`.

## The Deletion Guard (why neither agent oversteps)

The risk the owner flagged: an agent treats a sync as "make both sides identical" and deletes a
design that was intentionally kept. These rules prevent that on both sides:

- App side: A sync only advances the submodule pointer. It performs no `rm`, no `git rm`, no
  file deletion, and never edits files under `design/`. If the design repo removed a component,
  the app agent flags it for the owner (and removes the corresponding production shell only on
  explicit owner instruction) — it never auto-deletes app code to "match."
- Design side (Replit, enforced by `design/DESIGN_BOUNDARY.md` + `sync-manifest.json`): The
  design agent copies/edits only files listed in `sync-manifest.json`, never deletes app-repo files,
  and lists retired components under "Deprecated components" in `DESIGN_BOUNDARY.md` rather than
  deleting them downstream.
- The `// design-sync` marker on line 1 of each mockup marks it design-owned. It is the design
  repo's signal of "this is mine to regenerate." The app never strips or relies on copying it,
  because the app does not vendor the files.

## Fixing Mockup Copy / Visuals: List It, Never Direct Edit

The app agent must not edit files under `design/` or push to the design remote (one-way rule).
When the app agent or owner spots a design issue (wrong copy, currency reference, color, missing
state), the app agent produces a precise, file-by-file list for the Replit design agent:

```
design/ list (for Replit)
- ServiceCredits.tsx:168 — remove "≈ $242 USD purchasing power…"; credits are not pegged to fiat.
- MobileServiceCredits.tsx:64 — remove "credits ≈ $242 USD".
```

Replit applies the fix in the design repo, commits with the manifest, and the app picks it up on the
next pointer bump. The app implements the corrected design afterward.

## Adding / Removing Designs (summary; full text in `design/DESIGN_BOUNDARY.md`)

- Add: Replit builds the component under `…/survivor-hub/`, adds `// design-sync` line 1, adds
  the filename to `sync-manifest.json`, commits both together. App picks it up on next sync.
- Remove: Replit deletes it from the design repo and the manifest, and notes it under
  "Deprecated components." The app removes the matching shell only on explicit owner instruction.

